Every mainstream language picks a lane. Python defines `__add__` / `__lt__` dunder methods on a class. Rust implements the `std::ops::Add` and `PartialOrd` traits. Go refuses operator overloading entirely and forces named methods.

```python
# Python: dunder methods on the class
class Vec2:
    def __add__(self, o): return Vec2(self.x + o.x, self.y + o.y)
v = a + b        # calls a.__add__(b)
```

```rust
// Rust: implement a std trait
impl Add for Vec2 {
    type Output = Vec2;
    fn add(self, o: Vec2) -> Vec2 { Vec2 { x: self.x + o.x, y: self.y + o.y } }
}
let v = a + b;
